This not only confirms the theory—previously accepted in scientific circles—that at least some dinosaurs had feathers, but also provides information that fossils generally cannot offer, such as color and three-dimensional arrangement.
Scientists say that the ornamental feathers of this animal are brownish chestnut in color on the upper side and carotenoid in color on the lower side.
This finding also enhances our understanding of feather evolution in birds.
Since the feathers of this dinosaur lack a well-developed central shaft, known as a rachis, but possess other feather features—barbs and fine filaments—researchers therefore argue that the evolutionary development of the shaft likely occurred later than the other feather characteristics.
The structure of the feathers indicates that they were not used for flight but rather for temperature regulation or display purposes. Researchers believe that even if this dinosaur was a juvenile, the specimen still represents a feather from an adult animal, not the down of a hatchling.
Researchers stated that even if this specimen is a young dinosaur, it still represents a fully grown animal, not a chick's down.
