The history of Taiwan dates back to the 15th century, when European navigators who passed by there named the island "Ilha Formosa," meaning "Beautiful Island."
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in southwestern Taiwan, marking the beginning of a transformation in local grain production and employing Chinese laborers to work on their sugar and rice farms.
In 1683, the Qing dynasty (1644–1912) forcibly took control of the western and northern regions of Taiwan, and in 1885 declared Taiwan as one of the provinces of the Qing Empire.
In 1895, after its defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki, under which control of Taiwan was ceded to Japan, and Japan maintained sovereignty over the island until 1945.
