The Great Pyramid of Giza is the only one of the Seven Wonders that still remains.
The Great Pyramid, built by the Egyptians in the third century BCE, is one of several large pyramidal structures constructed in honor of a deceased pharaoh.
The Giza Plateau, or the "Giza Pyramid Complex," in the Valley of the Kings in Egypt includes several pyramids (among which the Great Pyramid is the largest), several small tombs, some temples, and the large statue of the Sphinx located in that valley.
The Great Pyramid was built in honor of the pharaoh Khufu, and many smaller pyramids, tombs, and temples were constructed in honor of his wives and family members.
The "upward arrow" symbol resembles a V, and the "downward arrow" symbol looks like a hook or a square without the bottom part.
Up means you should start from the toe and pull the arch, while down means you should start from the heel (which is the same point where the hand grips the arch) and push the arch forward.
