The modern city of Casablanca was founded by Berber fishermen in the 10th century BCE, and the Phoenicians, Romans, and Merenids later used it as a strategic port, calling it Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed it and then rebuilt it under the name of Casablanca, and only abandoned it after the earthquake of 1755.
The Sultan of Morocco rebuilt the city of Dar al-Beida, and the Spanish merchants who established their trading posts there named it Casablanca.
Casablanca is one of the cities in Morocco whose shopping locations have the least appeal.
Around the old Medina, you can find places selling traditional Moroccan goods, such as tagines, pottery, leather items, hookahs, and a full range of patterns, but all of these are merely for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in the east, near Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by volcanic eruptions from Mount Nyiragongo, which buried most of the city's streets, especially the city center.
Although security in Goma has been established with accountability, any visit outside Goma should be carefully assessed to gain awareness of the ongoing conflict situation in North Kivu province.
This town also serves as a base for climbing Mount Nyiragongo, along the route to the most accessible gorilla trekking sites in Africa.
You can use motorcycle taxis to get around the city of Goma. The usual local price for a short ride is around 500 Congolese francs.
The city of "Timbuktu," which had almost no access, has led to its name being used as a metaphor for strange, remote places.
Today, Timbuktu is a poor town, although due to its historical significance it has tourist appeal and even has an airport.
In 1990, it was added to the List of World Heritage in Danger due to the threat posed by desertification.
That stop was one of the main stops during Louis Gates' special PBS program titled "Africa's Wonders."
This city is clearly at odds with other cities in this country, as it is predominantly characterized by Arab features rather than African ones.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa, stretching from the eastern border with Mozambique and along the northern border with Zimbabwe, and its southern boundary is the Crocodile River.
This park covers an area of 19,500 square kilometers and is divided into 14 distinct biogeographical realms, each supporting different wildlife.
One of the main attractions of South Africa, and considered the national flower of South Africa's National Parks (SANParks).
As with all national parks in South Africa, daily conservation and entry to this park also incur a fee.
Buying a game park pass that provides access to selected parks in South Africa or all of South Africa's national parks can be beneficial.
Hong Kong Island has given its name to the territory of Hong Kong and is a place that many tourists consider the main focus of their visit.
The skyline of Hong Kong, composed of towering buildings, resembles a bright bar chart clearly visible alongside the waters of Victoria Harbour.
To get the best view of Hong Kong, leave the island and head toward the opposite shore of Kowloon.
The majority of Hong Kong Island's urban development along the northern coastal line has been densely concentrated on reclaimed, developed land.
This is a place that was seized by the British colonizers for themselves, so if you're looking for evidence of the colonial past of this land, this is a good place to start.
Sundarbans is the world's largest continuous mangrove forest, stretching 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the coasts of Bangladesh and India inland.
Sundarbans has been designated as a UNESCO World Heritage site. The forested area located within the Indian territory of the Sundarbans Forest Park is called Sundarbans.
These forests are not just the swamps of Karna—they also contain some of the last remaining remnants of the vast forests that once covered the Sindh and Ganges plains.
The Sundarbans cover an area of approximately 3,850 square kilometers, with about one-third of it consisting of water/swamps.
Sundarbans has been a wildlife sanctuary since 1966, and it is estimated that there are now 400 royal Bengal tigers and around 30,000 deer in this region.
Buses depart throughout the day from the inter-district station (opposite the river), but most of them, especially those heading east or to Jakaar/Bumteng, operate between 06:30 and 07:30.
Since intercity buses are often full, it is recommended to book tickets a few days in advance.
