Shared taxis are one of the fast and convenient ways to travel to nearby locations such as Paro (Nu 150) and Punakha (Nu 200).
The Oiapoque River Bridge is a cable bridge. This bridge spans the Oiapoque River to connect the cities of Oiapoque in Brazil and Saint-Georges de l'Oyapock in French Guiana.
The height of the two towers is 83 meters, the length is 378 meters, and it has two lanes, each 3.50 meters wide.
The vertical clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Construction was completed in August 2011, and it became operational for passage and use in March 2017.
The bridge is scheduled for completion in September 2017, which is the expected time for completing the customs stations.
The Guaraní were the most significant indigenous group inhabiting present-day eastern Paraguay, living a semi-nomadic lifestyle based on hunting and practicing subsistence agriculture.
Chaco was the residence area for other indigenous groups such as the Guaycurú and Payaguá, who sustained themselves through hunting, gathering, and fishing.
In the 16th century, Paraguay, which had previously been known as the "Vast Province of the Indies," came into existence as a result of the encounters between Spanish conquistadors and native groups.
The Spanish began the colonial period, which lasted three centuries.
Since the establishment of Asunción in 1537, Paraguay has managed to preserve many of its indigenous characteristics and traits.
Argentina is renowned for having one of the best futsal teams and the best players in the world.
The biggest event of the year takes place in December at the chogan fields of Las Cañitas.
Tournaments and smaller competitions can also be seen here at other times of the year.
For information about tournament news and ticket purchase locations for the Chogán tournaments, please visit Asociacion Argentina de Polo.
The official currency of the Falkland Islands is the Falkland Islands pound (FKP), which is set at the same value as the British pound (GBP).
In the only bank located in the islands, situated in Stanley and along the FIC West shop, currency exchange is available.
The British pound is usually accepted throughout the islands, and Visa and American dollar cards are also commonly accepted.
Credit cards may not be accepted in more remote islands, although British and U.S. currency are generally accepted; be sure to coordinate the accepted payment method with the owners in advance.
Foreign currency exchange on the Falkland Islands is almost impossible outside the islands, so exchange your money before leaving.
Since Montevideo is located in the Southern Hemisphere, when it is winter in the Northern Hemisphere, it is summer there, and vice versa.
Montevideo has a subtropical climate; during the summer months, temperatures usually rise above 30 degrees Celsius.
Winter can be deceptively cold: temperatures rarely drop below freezing, but wind and humidity work together to make you feel colder than the thermometer indicates.
There is no distinct dry or rainy season: the amount of rainfall remains almost constant throughout the year.
Although many animals in the park are accustomed to seeing people, wild animals are still wild and should not be fed or disturbed.
According to park authorities, keep a distance of at least 100 meters from bears and wolves, and 25 meters from other wild animals!
American bison, deer, elk, bears, and nearly all large animals, no matter how tame they may seem, can attack.
Every year, dozens of visitors are injured due to not maintaining a safe distance. These are large, wild, and inherently dangerous animals, so please do not enter their space.
In addition, keep in mind that odors attract bears and other wild animals, so avoid carrying or cooking fragrant foods, and keep your campsite clean.
Apia is the capital city of Samoa. This town is located on the island of Upolu and has a population of slightly less than 40,000 people.
Apia was established in the 1850s and has been the official capital of Samoa since 1959.
This port is famous for the incident in 1889 when a fleet of seven ships from Germany, the United States, and Britain refused to leave the port.
Besides one British warship, all the ships sank, and nearly 200 Americans and Germans lost their lives.
While striving for independence under the organization of the Mau movement, a peaceful gathering in the city resulted in the killing of the senior leader "Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III."
Due to Auckland's location between two harbors, there are many coastlines. The most popular ones are located in three areas.
The North Shore (in the North Harbor area) of the Pacific Ocean stretches from Long Beach in the north to Downey in the south.
They are almost all sandy beaches where swimming is safe, and most of them have shade from pohutukawa trees.
The Tamaki Drive coastline in Whitemata Harbour is located in the suburbs of the well-known areas of Māngere and Saint Heliers in central Auckland.
These are family-friendly beaches, which can sometimes be crowded and feature good shops along the shoreline. Swimming is safe.
The main local beer is called "Number One," which is not a complex beer, but rather enjoyable and refreshing. The other local beer is called "Manta."
There are many French wines available for consumption, but wines from New Zealand and Australia may be better preserved during transport.
Drinking local tap water is completely safe, but if you're concerned, you can easily find bottled water.
For Australians, the "flat white" is an unfamiliar concept. A "short black" is the same as an "espresso," cappuccinos are served with plenty of froth (not just a little foam on top), and tea is served without milk.
The hot chocolate is up to Belgian standards. The fruit juices are expensive but excellent.
