Breaking waves are the backwash currents of waves that move from the shore toward the sea, which usually occur on rocks or similar structures.
The reverse current, due to the underwater topology, concentrates in several deeper sections, and a fast current toward the deep water may occur there.
Most drownings occur due to exhaustion from struggling to swim against the wave's current, which can be impossible.
As soon as you leave the current, swimming back against normal conditions won't be any harder.
Try to aim for a place where you won't get caught again, or, depending on your skills and whether or not your presence has been noticed, you might be able to wait for a rescue operation.
The shock of returning home occurs earlier than cultural shock (it happens earlier than the honeymoon phase), lasts longer, and can be more intense.
Travelers who easily adapt to a new culture sometimes find it very difficult to return to their native culture again.
Upon returning home after living abroad, you have adopted a new culture and have lost some of your habits from your homeland's culture.
At the beginning of their foreign trip, people were probably patient and understanding, knowing that travelers who have arrived in a new country need time to adjust.
People might not expect that patience and understanding of circumstances are equally essential for travelers returning home.
