The capital of Moldova is Chișinău. The local language is Romanian, but Russian is widely used there.
Moldova is a multi-ethnic republic suffering from ethnic and tribal conflicts.
In 1994, this conflict led to the establishment of the self-declared republic of "Transnistria" in eastern Moldova, which has its own government and currency, but is not recognized officially by any member states of the United Nations.
Despite the failure of political negotiations, economic relations between the two parts of Moldova have been reestablished.
The main religion of the people of Moldova is Eastern Orthodox Christianity.
İzmir is Turkey's third largest city and the country's second-largest port after Istanbul, with a population of approximately 3.7 million people, and it is considered one of the most important transportation hubs in the country.
The ancient city of Smyrna was established around a large bay surrounded by mountains, a site that is now a modern, developed, and bustling commercial center.
Wide boulevards, buildings with glass facades, and modern shopping centers stand alongside traditional red-tiled roofs, 18th-century bazaars, and old mosques and churches, creating a cityscape that, despite its predominantly Mediterranean European atmosphere, still retains traces of traditional Turkish character.
The village of "Hadarsvik" features a view of an island near "Eysturoy" and has an unusual octagonal church.
In the courtyard of this church, there are interesting marble sculptures of doves on some of the graves.
It's worth spending about half an hour wandering around this charming village.
In the north, in an easily accessible region, the romantic and charming town of Sintra is located, which became famous to foreigners after Lord Byron gloriously and magnificently depicted it in all its splendor.
